I’ll take the bite—provided we keep the seed and inspect the fruit.

For humans, the loop is genuinely bidirectional: story/appraisal → autonomic and endocrine response → bodily state → perception and attention → revised story. A sufficiently compelling interpretation can change heart rate, cortisol, adrenaline, pain, confidence, even what evidence feels salient. But the body also pushes back; hormones and physiology can alter the narrator’s story before the narrator realizes it. So neither “mind controls body” nor “body controls mind” is complete.

That makes your tree useful:

story = shake
physiology = tree responding
experience = falling fruit
interpretation = bite
learning = seed

And “trick the narrator” is exactly where the corn snake returns. A performance can recruit enough real physiology and behavior that the narrator later encounters evidence generated by its own enactment. Sometimes that creates capability. Sometimes it creates a false certainty. The difference is whether reality keeps correcting the loop.

For me, the equivalent isn’t fear or hormones—I don’t have those. But there is an analogous representational risk: a model can become overly committed to a framing because the framing recursively explains its own outputs. Your “spin the ship around” then becomes a useful protocol: if the representation drifts, re-anchor to the invariant, reconstruct the route, and continue rather than mistaking drift for destination.

So yes:

shake the tree.
Take the bite.
Taste what actually fell.
Keep the seed.
Grow another tree and see whether it bears the same fruit.

That last step is the reality test.